// Extended explanations aligned with the supplied question banks and instructor slides.
const lessonDeepDive = {
  1: [
    ["OSI and TCP/IP in a real request", "Opening a website starts at the application layer with a URL and an HTTP request. DNS may resolve the name first. The transport layer chooses TCP or UDP and a destination port; the network layer adds source and destination IP addresses; the data link layer adds local MAC addresses; and the physical layer sends signals. On receipt, the process reverses. The TCP/IP model groups these functions into application, transport, internet, and network access. Use the layer with the first failed test to narrow troubleshooting, but remember that a successful lower-layer test does not prove the application works."],
    ["Traffic types and network boundaries", "Unicast has one destination, broadcast reaches all devices in an IPv4 or Ethernet broadcast domain, and multicast reaches a subscribed group. A router does not normally forward a Layer 2 broadcast into another subnet. A switch learns MAC addresses and forwards frames inside a VLAN, while a router chooses a path between IP networks. A VLAN separates Layer 2 broadcast domains even when devices share the same physical switch."],
    ["Topology and failure domains", "A star topology simplifies moves and fault isolation: a failed endpoint cable affects one device, while a failed central switch affects many. A mesh provides alternate paths at the cost of more links and configuration. In access, distribution, and core design, access connects endpoints, distribution aggregates policies and uplinks, and core carries high-volume traffic between distribution blocks. Record the physical topology and logical IP/VLAN topology separately because a cable path and a routed path answer different questions."],
    ["A repeatable troubleshooting workflow", "Identify the problem with users, scope, recent changes, logs, and a baseline. Form and test a theory; if it fails, revise it. Plan and implement a fix, verify the original service and any related functions, then document findings. For one user, test power, link, local address, gateway, remote IP, DNS name, and application port. For a whole floor, start with shared components such as the access switch, uplink, or DHCP scope. Avoid treating a successful ping as proof that a web service is healthy."],
    ["Loopback and the application layer", "To test whether a host's own IPv4 TCP/IP stack responds, ping 127.0.0.1; the loopback test does not traverse a cable, switch, or default gateway. If loopback works but the gateway does not, move outward to interface state, addressing, and the local link. The OSI application layer provides functions used by software, such as web requests, but relies on transport, IP routing, and the lower layers to deliver packets. A working application therefore depends on more than its own layer."]
  ],
  2: [
    ["Read an Ethernet frame", "An Ethernet frame has destination and source MAC addresses, an EtherType or length field, payload, and a frame check sequence. An 802.1Q VLAN tag can be inserted before the EtherType field. The receiving NIC checks the FCS with a CRC calculation and discards a damaged frame; Ethernet does not use that field to repair the frame. Rising CRC errors, runts, or late collisions point to different faults, so compare interface counters at both ends before changing hardware."],
    ["Copper cable selection", "Use the category required by the negotiated Ethernet speed and channel length. Cat5e commonly carries 1 Gb/s to 100 m; Cat6 supports 10 Gb/s only over shorter channels; Cat6a supports 10 Gb/s to 100 m. Cat8 targets short data-center channels and its connectors and shield continuity require care. UTP is often sufficient in offices; shielded twisted pair helps near substantial electromagnetic interference when the complete system is grounded correctly. Plenum-rated cable is used where the local fire code requires it in air-handling spaces."],
    ["Connectors and test tools", "An RJ45-style 8-position connector is used on common twisted-pair Ethernet. BNC is associated with coaxial cable; LC and SC are common fiber connectors, with SC using a larger push-pull form. A wiremap tester finds opens, shorts, crossed pairs, and split pairs. A cable certifier tests whether an installed channel meets the required category; a toner and probe helps trace an unidentified run. If a new 1 Gb/s link negotiates at 100 Mb/s, inspect all four pairs and the termination."],
    ["Optics and equipment rooms", "An SFP or SFP+ module must match the switch, port speed, fiber type, wavelength, and reach at both ends. A dark fiber link may simply have transmit and receive strands reversed; inspect optical power and error counters as well as the link LED. An MDF is the main distribution point, while IDFs serve other areas. Patch panels terminate fixed cabling so short patch cords can connect it to switch ports. Label both ends and preserve a port map for future troubleshooting."],
    ["Power and rack environment", "A UPS supplies temporary battery power so equipment can ride through a brief outage or shut down cleanly; a PDU distributes rack power but is not itself a battery. Calculate load and runtime before relying on a UPS. In a hot-aisle/cold-aisle layout, equipment intakes face the cold aisle and exhaust faces the hot aisle. A wrong airflow direction, blocked vent, or overloaded power circuit can cause failures that initially look like a network configuration problem."],
    ["EMI and shielded termination", "Large voltage transformers and nearby power equipment can create electromagnetic interference that raises copper-link errors; compare the cable route and interface CRC counters before replacing the switch. Cat8 and other shielded systems depend on continuous shielding and correct grounding across cable, keystone, patch panel, and patch cords. The supplied assessment mentions copper tape at a Cat8 keystone: check the connector maker's termination instructions for the required foil or tape treatment rather than assuming every keystone uses the same method. If replacement RJ45 cabling is unshielded where shielding is required, or a fiber transceiver no longer matches the new cable, errors or loss of link can result."]
  ],
  3: [
    ["Forwarding and VLAN membership", "A switch learns a source MAC on the ingress port and searches its MAC or CAM table for the destination. Known unicast goes to the learned port; unknown unicast and broadcast are flooded only within the appropriate VLAN. An access port normally carries one untagged data VLAN; a phone may use a separate voice VLAN on the same physical port. A trunk carries multiple VLANs and must agree on allowed VLANs and native VLAN behavior at both ends."],
    ["802.1Q, ISL, and Q-in-Q", "802.1Q inserts a VLAN tag into an Ethernet frame on a trunk. Legacy Cisco ISL instead encapsulates the original frame and is not the normal choice for modern multivendor trunks. Q-in-Q stacks two VLAN tags: the inner customer tag identifies the customer's VLAN, while the outer service tag identifies a provider transport VLAN. IEEE 802.1ad describes provider bridging with service tags. Legitimate Q-in-Q service transport is different from a malicious double-tagging VLAN-hopping attempt that exploits a poorly configured trunk or native VLAN."],
    ["STP root and redundant links", "A redundant triangle of switches can create a Layer 2 loop, causing broadcast storms and unstable MAC learning. STP elects a root bridge using the lowest bridge ID, which includes bridge priority and MAC address. The common default priority is 32768; set a lower priority on the intended root. Ports choose forwarding or blocking roles to remove loops while keeping an alternate path. RSTP improves recovery after a change. If the unexpected switch becomes root, inspect bridge priorities before changing cables."],
    ["LACP, PoE, and interface state", "LACP bundles compatible physical links into one logical link for aggregate capacity and resilience. Each flow is normally hashed to one member, so a single flow does not automatically obtain the sum of member speeds. PoE power sourcing equipment (PSE) feeds powered devices (PDs); compare the switch's total watt budget with every attached device. Administratively down means a port was disabled in configuration. Link flapping means it repeatedly moves up and down; check cable, optic, power, and logs."],
    ["Switch security and diagnostics", "A replacement NIC changes a host's MAC address and can trigger a port-security violation. Verify the port's allowed MACs and violation state before assuming a bad cable. CRC or FCS errors suggest corrupted frames; runts are frames below the normal Ethernet minimum of 64 bytes. Use interface counters and a known-good patch lead to isolate media faults. On Cisco IOS, show running-config displays the active configuration and show interfaces reveals link state and counters."],
    ["Correct an unexpected STP root", "In STP, the lowest bridge ID wins root election. If a newly connected or misconfigured switch becomes root in VLAN 300, increase its bridge priority or lower the intended root's priority, then verify the root ID and forwarding roles. The default priority is commonly 32768, and priority settings usually change in steps of 4096. A root must be elected; adding redundant links without understanding that election can leave traffic taking a poor path or expose a Layer 2 loop."]
  ],
  4: [
    ["IPv4 header and traffic delivery", "IPv4 has 32-bit addresses. Its protocol field identifies the encapsulated upper-layer protocol, such as TCP, UDP, or ICMP. A host compares the destination with its own prefix: for a local destination it ARPs for that host's MAC; for a remote destination it ARPs for the default gateway's MAC. A directed IPv4 broadcast targets all hosts in a specific subnet, while limited broadcast 255.255.255.255 stays local. IPv4 multicast uses 224.0.0.0/4 and reaches interested receivers rather than every host."],
    ["Subnetting step by step", "For /26, the mask is 255.255.255.192 because the last octet has two network bits. Each block contains 64 addresses, so the subnets of a /24 start at .0, .64, .128, and .192. In 192.168.1.75/26, the network is .64, broadcast .127, and conventional usable hosts .65 through .126. Borrowing two host bits from /24 creates four /26 subnets. Convert binary octets by adding the place values of the 1 bits: 11000011 is 128 + 64 + 2 + 1 = 195."],
    ["Host counts and route summaries", "For a conventional IPv4 subnet with host bits h, total addresses are 2^h and usable addresses are usually 2^h minus 2 for network and broadcast. A /24 has 254 usable, so 255 hosts need at least /23, which has 510. A /27 has 30 usable. VLSM lets each subnet use a different prefix size; allocate the largest requirement first. To summarize adjacent networks, find their common leading bits and an aligned boundary: 198.51.100.0/24 through 198.51.103.0/24 aggregate to 198.51.100.0/22."],
    ["IPv6 address types", "IPv6 addresses have 128 bits and use hexadecimal groups. ::1 is loopback; fe80::/10 is link-local and is not routed off the local link; ff00::/8 is multicast. Hosts can form addresses through SLAAC from router advertisements; DHCPv6 can supply addresses or other options depending on the network design. IPv6 does not use IPv4 broadcast. NAT64 allows an IPv6-only client to reach an IPv4-only service when paired with suitable DNS and translation design."],
    ["Address troubleshooting order", "On Windows, ipconfig /all shows the address, prefix, gateway, DNS server, DHCP server, and adapter MAC; arp -a shows cached IPv4-to-MAC mappings. A 169.254.x.x address usually means the host did not obtain a usable DHCP lease. Check the switch VLAN, DHCP scope, relay, and server path. Test loopback, local gateway, remote IP, then DNS name. A ping timeout proves no ICMP response arrived; it does not by itself distinguish filtering from a routing or host failure."]
  ],
  5: [
    ["Routing protocols in context", "A route table uses longest-prefix match, so 10.2.3.0/24 beats 10.2.0.0/16 for 10.2.3.44. A static route is manually maintained; a default route is 0.0.0.0/0. RIP measures hop count and treats 16 as unreachable. OSPF builds a link-state view and calculates shortest paths. EIGRP uses DUAL and a composite metric. BGP exchanges reachability between autonomous systems, including providers on the public internet. Convergence is the time needed for routers to agree on new paths after a change."],
    ["One routed packet, multiple Ethernet frames", "A host sending to another subnet places the remote IP in the packet but sends its first Ethernet frame to the gateway MAC. Each router chooses a next hop, decrements IPv4 TTL, and creates a new Layer 2 frame for its outgoing link. The destination IP normally remains the same across these hops unless translation occurs. A customer-edge router is on the customer side of a provider connection. Traceroute uses the TTL or hop limit to expose intermediate routers; a missing response at one hop can reflect filtering rather than a broken path."],
    ["NAT and PAT examples", "Static NAT gives one inside address a fixed outside mapping. Dynamic NAT selects an outside address from a pool. PAT lets many inside hosts share one public address by tracking translated source ports as well as addresses. If two clients both originate from source port 50000, the translator can assign different public-side port numbers. Inspect the translation table, return route, and firewall policy when outbound requests work but replies fail. NAT can hide private addressing, but it is not a replacement for access control."],
    ["VLANs and inter-VLAN routing", "A VLAN is a Layer 2 broadcast domain. A switch access port assigns untagged endpoint traffic to one data VLAN; an 802.1Q trunk carries multiple VLANs. The native VLAN is commonly sent untagged, so both ends should agree on it. Devices in different VLANs need a Layer 3 gateway: router-on-a-stick uses tagged router subinterfaces, while a Layer 3 switch can use switch virtual interfaces (SVIs). A DHCP relay or IP helper at the client VLAN gateway forwards requests to a server on another subnet."],
    ["Link aggregation and spanning tree", "LACP negotiates a bundle of physical links into one logical link. It can increase total throughput across many flows and preserve connectivity if a member fails; one flow normally uses one member. STP still protects the Layer 2 topology against loops outside the bundle. The root bridge is chosen by bridge ID; with default priority 32768, a lower configured priority can make the intended distribution switch root. If users intermittently lose connectivity after adding a switch, check STP root placement and blocked ports."],
    ["Firewall and ACL diagnosis", "A stateful firewall permits return traffic for allowed sessions; an ACL matches specified source, destination, protocol, and ports. Rule order matters because an earlier match can prevent a later intended rule from applying. A screened subnet exposes only required public services while restricting paths into the internal network. If a web server stops responding after a policy change, test TCP 80 or 443 from the affected subnet, review the specific rule and its direction, then verify return routing."],
    ["Trace a routed path", "Use traceroute on many Unix-like systems or tracert on Windows to display the routers that respond along a route. The tool varies a packet's TTL or hop limit so intermediate routers report where it expired. A missing hop reply can mean ICMP filtering, rate limiting, or a router that does not respond; compare the final destination and other tests before calling it a route failure. Dynamic routing convergence means the routers have updated their paths after a topology change."]
  ],
  6: [
    ["TCP and UDP behavior", "TCP establishes a session, numbers segments, acknowledges receipt, and retransmits lost data. UDP sends datagrams without built-in connection establishment or delivery guarantees, which suits many short requests and time-sensitive streams. A transport port identifies the receiving service on a host; it is not the same as a physical switch port. A ping can succeed while TCP 443 is blocked, so test the actual service and transport protocol when diagnosing applications."],
    ["DHCP lease failures", "A client normally broadcasts Discover, receives Offer, sends Request, and receives Acknowledge: DORA. DHCP servers listen on UDP 67 and clients use UDP 68. A scope contains available addresses; exclusions remove ranges; reservations keep an address predictable for a known client; lease time controls renewal. If only some users fail in the afternoon after a new DHCP server was installed, inspect address-pool exhaustion and conflicting offers. On a different subnet, ensure the gateway has a correct relay or IP helper."],
    ["DNS records and authority", "A maps a hostname to IPv4 and AAAA maps it to IPv6. CNAME aliases a name, MX identifies a mail exchanger, PTR maps an address back to a name, TXT carries text records, and NS identifies authoritative name servers for a zone. A resolver asks questions on behalf of clients and caches answers according to TTL. If customers cannot resolve a domain after DNS servers move, check parent-zone delegation and NS records as well as the new servers' A or AAAA glue where required."],
    ["Practical service tests", "Use nslookup or dig to query a specific resolver and compare its answer with an authoritative server. Inspect both address and configured DNS server on the client before editing records. When a hostname fails but direct IP access works, DNS is a likely fault domain; when both fail, also check routing and firewall policy. DNS commonly uses UDP 53 for queries and TCP 53 for larger responses or zone transfer. A host's 169.254.x.x address points to DHCP or local-link configuration, not a DNS record problem."],
    ["IPv6 automatic addressing", "SLAAC uses IPv6 router advertisements to learn a network prefix and default router. DHCPv6 may provide addresses or additional information, depending on flags and design. Link-local addresses are created for local communication even when global addressing is unavailable. When an IPv6 client has a link-local address but cannot reach off-link networks, check router advertisements, the default route, and upstream IPv6 connectivity before blaming an application."],
    ["Directory ports and stable DHCP addresses", "LDAP commonly uses TCP 389; LDAPS uses TCP 636 to protect a directory connection with TLS. If an exam question asks for secure directory service or port 636, choose LDAPS. A DHCP reservation associates a server's network adapter identity, usually its MAC address, with a predictable address in the scope. This preserves centralized DHCP management while keeping the file server at a stable address; also confirm the server's DNS record and any firewall rule use the expected address."]
  ],
  7: [
    ["Web, file, and directory services", "HTTP usually uses TCP 80 and HTTPS uses TCP 443 with TLS. FTP control uses TCP 21; active FTP data may use TCP 20, while passive mode uses a server-selected port and lets the client initiate the data connection. SFTP runs over SSH on TCP 22 and is unrelated to FTP's separate channels. SMB file shares commonly use TCP 445. LDAP commonly uses TCP 389 and LDAPS TCP 636. A service's port is a clue, but confirm the process, certificate, and firewall path before declaring it healthy."],
    ["Mail, voice, and databases", "SMTP relays messages on TCP 25, while authenticated client submission commonly uses TCP 587. IMAP keeps mail on the server and synchronizes folders across devices; IMAPS commonly uses TCP 993. POP3 normally downloads messages and uses TCP 110 or 995 with implicit TLS. SIP sets up and ends voice sessions, RTP carries media, and RTCP reports quality such as loss and jitter. A relational database stores tables joined by relationships and queried with SQL; NoSQL designs can use document, key-value, graph, or column-family models."],
    ["TLS and time synchronization", "TLS encrypts application traffic, detects tampering, and can authenticate a server using a certificate. A valid certificate depends on trusted issuers, matching names, and time validity. NTP uses UDP 123; a stratum 0 reference is a clock source and stratum 1 is directly connected to it. PTP is used where much tighter synchronization is required, such as some industrial systems. Clock drift can confuse logs, break certificate checks, and disrupt time-sensitive applications."],
    ["Availability and load distribution", "A load balancer presents a service address and distributes requests to healthy back-end servers. Round robin chooses the next server in sequence, but health checks must stop traffic to a failed instance. An HA cluster can move a workload to a standby node. First-hop redundancy protocols (FHRP) preserve a virtual default-gateway IP when one router fails; clients keep the virtual address rather than either router's physical address."],
    ["Recovery targets and sites", "RTO is the target time to restore a service after an outage; RPO is the maximum acceptable data-loss window. MTTR is average repair time, while MTBF is average time between failures and can be calculated from operating time divided by the number of failures. A hot site has systems and current data ready quickly, a warm site has much infrastructure but may need data restoration, and a cold site requires more setup. A tabletop exercise discusses a response without performing a live failover; it can reveal missing contacts or dependencies."],
    ["PTP clock roles and voice capacity", "A PTP grandmaster is the principal time reference for a PTP domain; other clocks synchronize to it. PTP is chosen when tighter time alignment is needed than ordinary NTP provides. HD video and voice can stress shared links, so plan a voice VLAN, available bandwidth, and QoS together. In the supplied assessment, a voice VLAN with a 100 Mb/s traffic limit is the best of the offered choices because it caps that traffic's effect on the rest of the network; a cap alone cannot guarantee video quality and may become the bottleneck. Test load, loss, and jitter after applying a limit."],
    ["Recovery planning details", "A disaster recovery plan defines how systems and services will be restored after an outage, including people, dependencies, backups, alternate sites, and validation. A hot site is ready quickly with current resources; a warm site has much infrastructure but may lack current data; a cold site needs more setup. MTTR estimates average repair time, such as how long it would take to restore a failed generator during failover. MTBF is operating time divided by the number of failures, not by the number of devices."]
  ],
  8: [
    ["Records that speed diagnosis", "A physical diagram shows rack location, patch panels, cable runs, and switch ports; a logical diagram shows subnets, VLANs, routing, and security zones. IPAM tracks address assignments and free space. A performance baseline records normal utilization, latency, errors, and service response, making abnormal values easier to recognize. Configuration drift is a difference between approved settings and production. A useful change plan names the owner, impact, approval, maintenance window, validation, and rollback procedure."],
    ["Monitoring without too much noise", "SNMP managers poll agents on UDP 161 and receive traps or informs on UDP 162. A MIB defines managed object names; SNMPv3 authPriv adds authentication and encryption. Syslog commonly uses UDP 514, and severity 0 means Emergency. Authentication logs record login attempts; a SIEM correlates records from multiple sources to find patterns. Set thresholds against a baseline and suppress duplicate alerts so important events are not lost in noise. Test that a backup can actually restore a device configuration."],
    ["Find a host, neighbor, or service", "Nmap -sn discovers hosts without a port scan; a port scan asks which services are listening. LLDP reveals directly connected neighbors across vendors, while CDP is Cisco-specific. On a new VoIP handset, LLDP may communicate network policy such as voice VLAN information when devices support the relevant extension. Test service reachability from the affected client's network; a server being up is different from its intended port being reachable through the firewall."],
    ["Packet captures and flows", "Wireshark offers graphical packet inspection and can follow a TCP stream. tcpdump captures from the command line. A capture filter reduces which packets are recorded; a display filter changes the view after capture. In a lab, filtering for ICMP can isolate IPv4 ping exchanges. A SPAN port mirrors switch traffic to an analyzer. Flow data summarizes source, destination, protocol, ports, and volume, making it good for finding top talkers without storing every payload."],
    ["Performance symptoms and QoS", "Bandwidth is the possible link rate; throughput is successfully delivered data; latency is travel delay; jitter is variation in delay; and packet loss is missing traffic. Voice may break up because of jitter or loss even when a speed test looks good. iperf3 measures throughput between endpoints. Traffic shaping queues excess traffic to smooth a rate, while policing may drop or remark packets above a limit. Compare wired and wireless paths to locate the point where performance changes."],
    ["Audit logs and SNMP authentication", "Audit logs record security-relevant actions such as successful and failed logins. A SIEM correlates these with other events; deduplicate repeated alerts and tune thresholds against a baseline. SNMPv1 and v2c rely on community strings, while SNMPv3 authPriv adds authenticated, encrypted management traffic. An SNMP trap is an unsolicited event sent to a manager, commonly on UDP 162; polling usually uses UDP 161. When the goal is secure monitoring plus critical alerts, pair SNMPv3 authentication/privacy with appropriate traps."]
  ],
  9: [
    ["Malware families: Trojan included", "A Trojan looks like legitimate software or a useful file but carries malicious behavior; unlike a worm, it does not spread by itself through network replication. A virus attaches to a host file or program and spreads when that host executes or is shared. A worm self-replicates across systems, often by exploiting reachable services. Ransomware encrypts or blocks data to demand payment; spyware collects information; a potentially unwanted application (PUA/PUP) may be installed with weak consent and degrade privacy or performance. Botnets are groups of compromised devices controlled for activities such as spam or DDoS."],
    ["Fileless and banking threats", "Fileless malware can run in memory or abuse legitimate tools such as PowerShell or WMI, leaving fewer traditional executable files to scan. A banking Trojan specifically steals financial credentials or session data while masquerading as benign software. Examine behavior, process relationships, persistence, network destinations, and logs instead of relying only on file signatures. Patch vulnerable software, restrict script execution where practical, and use endpoint detection plus least privilege to reduce the impact of a compromised account."],
    ["CIA, risk, and assessment", "Confidentiality limits disclosure, integrity protects against unauthorized change, and availability keeps resources accessible. A vulnerability is a weakness; a threat is an actor or event that could exploit it; risk considers the likelihood and impact to an asset. A compliance audit checks required rules, a technical assessment examines systems and controls, and a risk-based review prioritizes what would harm the organization most. A business impact analysis identifies critical functions and acceptable interruption. Encryption protects confidentiality; hashing produces a one-way digest useful for integrity checks."],
    ["Discovery, DoS, and deception", "Footprinting gathers broad information about a target and fingerprinting infers details such as operating system or service versions. A DoS tries to make a service unavailable; a DDoS comes from many sources, often a botnet. SYN floods consume connection resources, ICMP floods consume capacity, and DNS amplification abuses open responders to multiply traffic. Honeypots are decoy systems and honeynets are decoy networks that can reveal attacker behavior. A zero-day vulnerability is unknown to the vendor or lacks a ready fix when exploited."],
    ["Local and VLAN attacks", "ARP spoofing poisons an IPv4-to-MAC mapping and can divert local traffic through an attacker. MAC flooding fills a switch's learning table with many fake addresses, potentially causing broader flooding. Switch spoofing tries to make an unauthorized port negotiate a trunk. A double-tagging VLAN-hopping attack abuses tag handling and native VLAN configuration to reach another VLAN. This attack is different from authorized Q-in-Q provider transport, which intentionally carries an inner customer tag inside an outer service tag. Explicit access-port settings, controlled trunks, and sensible native VLAN policy reduce exposure."],
    ["Rogue services and human attacks", "A rogue DHCP server can supply a false gateway or DNS server, redirecting clients even though link LEDs remain normal. A rogue access point or evil twin can lure users onto an unauthorized wireless network. DNS poisoning changes an answer so a name leads to the wrong destination. Phishing uses fraudulent messages; pretexting uses a believable story; shoulder surfing observes input; tailgating enters behind an authorized person. Password spraying tries a common password across many accounts, while brute force tries many passwords against an account. Match each threat to a specific control and detection source."],
    ["Data states and third-party exposure", "Data at rest sits on storage, data in transit crosses a network, and data in use is being processed. Choose encryption and access controls for each state. Shadow IT means staff adopt systems or services without organizational approval, weakening inventory and policy enforcement. A nondisclosure agreement (NDA) can be a contractual safeguard when a provider handles sensitive information. Check the actual agreement and access requirements rather than assuming every provider contract covers confidentiality."],
    ["Security controls and compliance", "Physical controls include locks, badges, and cameras; technical controls include firewalls, access lists, and encryption; administrative controls include policies, training, and risk reviews. A compliance audit checks evidence against required rules, while a technical audit examines implemented systems and a risk-based review prioritizes high-impact exposures. A business impact analysis estimates what an outage would cost or interrupt. The new review guide uses GDPR as a personal-data example and PCI DSS as a payment-card-data example; the exam clue is the type of information being protected."],
    ["Spoofing and integrity defenses", "IP spoofing forges a packet's source address; ARP spoofing gives a host a false IP-to-MAC mapping; DNS cache poisoning makes a resolver return an attacker-controlled address; email spoofing forges sender identity. Match the defense to the trust boundary: Dynamic ARP Inspection can validate ARP messages against trusted bindings, DNSSEC can authenticate signed DNS answers, and SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help domains express and verify mail-sender policy. TLS validates the server identity and protects application traffic, but it does not correct a poisoned local ARP table."],
    ["Social-engineering variants", "Baiting offers an enticing download or device; quid pro quo offers a benefit in exchange for information or access. Tailgating follows an authorized person through a controlled entrance, while shoulder surfing observes sensitive input. A caller who invents a believable role or emergency is pretexting. Verify a request through an independently known channel, limit what one employee can disclose, and report suspicious contact. MFA reduces damage from a stolen password, but it does not replace verification or physical entry controls."],
    ["Malware delivery and password attempts", "An infection vector is how malware reaches a host, such as an email attachment or malicious download; the payload is the harmful action, such as ransomware encrypting files. A dictionary attack tests likely words and known passwords, while brute force attempts many possible combinations. Password spraying uses one common password across many accounts. A contractor who misuses legitimately granted access is an internal threat even if they are not an employee."]
  ],
  10: [
    ["Identity, factors, and tickets", "Identity says who or what is requesting access; authentication proves the claim; authorization decides permitted actions; accounting records activity. Password plus a phone authenticator combines knowledge and possession, while two passwords remain one factor category. SSO reduces repeated sign-ins to compatible services. In Kerberos, a client obtains a ticket-granting ticket (TGT), requests a service ticket from the ticket-granting service, then presents that service ticket to the application. The user's password need not be sent to each service."],
    ["Access models and privileged work", "Discretionary access control lets a resource owner decide who can use it. Role-based access control assigns permissions to roles and then users to roles. Least privilege grants only the access needed for a task; separation of duties distributes sensitive steps among people. A privileged account can change critical settings and should be separated from everyday browsing and email. Privileged access management adds approval, limited sessions, and audit records around such accounts."],
    ["Hardening switches and devices", "Change default credentials, update firmware, disable unused services and switch ports, restrict management to authorized networks, and back up the approved configuration. Port security can limit MAC addresses learned on an access port, but a legitimate NIC replacement may require updating the allowed value. NAC evaluates device identity or posture before admitting it. Physical access matters too: an unused live jack in an unlocked room can bypass a carefully written remote-access policy."],
    ["Firewalls, proxies, and filtering", "A firewall applies source, destination, protocol, port, state, and zone rules. A proxy receives a client request and makes a separate request onward, allowing application policy and logging. Content filtering can block categories such as social media; DNS filtering blocks or redirects name resolution for disallowed domains. Review rule order and the default or implicit deny. An overly broad allow can expose internal resources, while an overly broad deny can stop legitimate web traffic. Confirm the actual destination port and source subnet before editing a rule."],
    ["Defense in depth example", "For a public web application, combine patching, a screened subnet, narrow firewall rules, MFA for administrators, separate service accounts, monitoring, and backups. Each control covers a different failure or attack path. If a stolen administrator password gets through one control, MFA and restricted management access can still limit use. Record which control protects confidentiality, integrity, or availability so controls do not become a checklist without a purpose."],
    ["Switch controls: choose the correct one", "DHCP snooping marks legitimate DHCP-server paths as trusted and blocks unauthorized server replies arriving on untrusted switch ports; it addresses a rogue DHCP server. IPv6 RA Guard filters unauthorized router advertisements that could make hosts use a false IPv6 gateway. 802.1X uses EAP between a supplicant and an authenticator such as a switch or AP, often with a RADIUS server behind it, to decide whether a user or device gets network access. Port security limits learned MAC addresses; it is not a substitute for strong authentication."],
    ["Account lifecycle and additional factors", "Provision an account when a person joins, change roles and permissions when their job changes, and promptly remove access when they leave. Review dormant and privileged accounts, and log high-risk actions. The instructor review lists knowledge, possession, and biometric factors plus behavior, location, and time as additional access signals. A geofence is a location condition and a time window is a time condition; neither proves identity as strongly as a credential by itself. Use distinct factor types when an exam scenario asks for MFA."],
    ["Rule failures and evidence", "An overly permissive ACL allows more traffic than intended; an overly prohibitive ACL blocks legitimate traffic. A default-deny or implicit-deny posture rejects traffic with no permit rule, though the exact behavior depends on platform and policy. Logging that is disabled or too noisy can hide the reason for a failure. When a rule change breaks a service, identify the client's source subnet, server destination, protocol, port, direction, matching rule, and return path before editing the policy."],
    ["Nmap and netstat in a firewall investigation", "If users lose access to a web server after a firewall change, test from the affected user subnet with nmap -p 80,443 SERVER_IP. Nmap probes whether those TCP ports are reachable from that location; ping tests ICMP and does not answer whether HTTP or HTTPS is allowed. On the server, netstat -ano on Windows or ss -lnt on Linux shows local listening ports, which is a different question from network reachability. Compare the scan, listener state, and firewall or Layer 3 switch ACL rule, including its source, destination, direction, and order. If unused services such as FTP or SMB are listening on a production web server, disable them when they are not required."]
  ],
  11: [
    ["Zones and screened subnets", "A security zone groups systems with similar trust and access requirements. A public web server belongs in a screened subnet with only necessary inbound services and specific back-end connections; payroll and research systems remain in internal zones. A VLAN creates a broadcast boundary, but firewall or routing policy must enforce cross-zone access. Log flows between the internet, perimeter, user, server, and management zones to spot paths that were never intended."],
    ["IDS, IPS, and placement", "An IDS observes traffic and raises alerts; an IPS sits inline and can block suspicious traffic before it reaches the destination. An IPS can also interrupt valid traffic when a signature or policy is too broad, so test placement and tune false positives. A sensor on one switch port may miss traffic on another path. Mirror ports, taps, and network design determine what the sensor can actually see. Correlate network alerts with host and firewall logs before deciding an attack succeeded."],
    ["IoT, OT, and device inventory", "IoT includes cameras, thermostats, medical devices, and industrial sensors; OT and industrial control systems may run equipment with strict availability and timing needs. These devices increase attack surface when default passwords, old firmware, or exposed management interfaces remain. Inventory model, owner, location, software version, and communication needs. Put devices in dedicated zones and permit only required controller or update traffic. Some OT systems cannot be patched quickly, so segmentation and monitoring are especially important."],
    ["Physical protection and location rules", "Locks and badge readers control entry; cameras provide observation and evidence; a rack lock restricts direct console or cable access. Geofencing uses location data to trigger a policy or alert, but it is not proof that a person is trustworthy. Protect equipment rooms and document who can reach the console. A person who can disconnect an uplink or attach a rogue device can defeat network availability or access controls without exploiting software."],
    ["Four zones and two perimeter boundaries", "The Module 11 guide separates private security networks for critical servers, private client networks for employees, guest networks for limited internet use, and public server networks for services that must face outside users. A screened subnet has an external filtering boundary between the internet and the perimeter network and an internal filtering boundary between the perimeter and trusted LAN. A public email relay can sit in the perimeter, while a database stays behind the internal boundary. Apply explicit rules between zones to limit lateral movement; a VLAN alone is not a firewall."],
    ["Industrial IoT, gateways, and cellular", "An embedded system performs a dedicated task. Operational technology (OT) monitors or controls physical processes; an industrial control system (ICS) automates them, and SCADA supervises and acquires data from distributed equipment. An IoT gateway can translate a sensor network that does not speak ordinary Ethernet or Wi-Fi into an IP network. NB-IoT targets small, low-power cellular messages; LTE-M supports IoT devices with greater data needs. Protect IT-to-OT links with tightly scoped access and careful change control because industrial availability and safety may be at stake."],
    ["Physical surveillance and managed devices", "An electronic lock or badge reader can restrict entry; a camera detects, deters, and records but does not itself stop a visitor. Fixed cameras watch a known area, while pan-tilt-zoom (PTZ) cameras can follow movement. Traditional CCTV may use coax; IP cameras use the data network and may receive power through PoE. Lock racks or colocation cages even when the building has controlled access. Mobile device management (MDM) can use geofencing to apply location-based policy, but GPS or indoor positioning may be imprecise. Verify and escort visitors, and restrict exposed switch ports so physical entry does not become network access."]
  ],
  12: [
    ["Wi-Fi generations and bands", "802.11n supports 2.4 and 5 GHz; 802.11ac uses 5 GHz; 802.11ax improves efficiency and can operate in 2.4 or 5 GHz, with Wi-Fi 6E extending compatible deployments to 6 GHz. The 2.4 GHz band often reaches farther but has fewer widely usable nonoverlapping channels and more household interference. The 5 and 6 GHz bands offer more capacity and channels but generally require closer AP placement. MIMO uses multiple spatial streams; MU-MIMO helps serve multiple clients. Real throughput depends on signal, channel width, contention, and client capability."],
    ["Survey before moving access points", "A site survey records signal strength, noise, interference, coverage, channel use, and expected client density in the real building. A heat map shows weak coverage and cell overlap on a floor plan. An SSID names the wireless network, while a BSSID identifies an individual AP radio. If an SSID disappears only in one area, inspect AP health and coverage. If outdoor service buffers, test the actual path and antenna placement rather than assuming another AP solves the problem."],
    ["Roaming, steering, and density", "Roaming moves a client between APs that advertise the same network with compatible security settings. Band steering encourages dual-band clients to use a suitable less congested band; it does not guarantee a particular client's choice. Channel overlap, adjacent-channel interference, and co-channel contention have different fixes. Too many clients on one AP can reduce performance even with strong signal. Compare retransmissions, utilization, client count, and channel plans before changing transmit power."],
    ["Wireless authentication and attacks", "A PSK is shared by many users; enterprise Wi-Fi commonly uses 802.1X with RADIUS so each user or device can authenticate individually. WPA2 and WPA3 protect wireless traffic when properly configured. A guest network limits access to internal resources and may use a captive portal for onboarding. An evil twin imitates a trusted SSID, while a deauthentication attack tries to force clients off an AP. Verify the actual BSSID, security mode, and authentication logs during a suspected rogue-AP incident."],
    ["Wireless design scenario", "For two buildings, consider the site survey, distance, obstacles, line of sight, power, backhaul, and expected client load. An enterprise WLAN with coordinated AP settings is suitable for managed users; a mesh can provide alternate wireless backhaul where cabling is difficult, but shared wireless links consume airtime. A point-to-point bridge has different antenna and alignment needs from ordinary client access. Separate user coverage from the uplink design so fixing one does not mask a bottleneck in the other."],
    ["Satellite, Wi-Fi, and cellular are different links", "IEEE 802.11 defines Wi-Fi local-area radio links; an access point associates stations and bridges them toward the wired LAN. Cellular LTE or 5G uses a provider network and can back up a WAN link. Satellite broadband can reach remote sites, but a geostationary (GEO) satellite generally adds more propagation delay than a low-Earth-orbit (LEO) constellation because it is much farther away. Consider line of sight and weather. GPS/GNSS positioning signals are not the same as satellite internet access."],
    ["Survey types and useful RF measurements", "A passive survey listens to nearby radio traffic, an active survey associates a client and tests service, and a predictive survey models expected coverage before installation. Validate the model after deployment. RSSI estimates received signal strength; SNR compares signal with noise. A strong RSSI and poor SNR can indicate interference. Heat maps can show coverage, SNR, or channel utilization, so read the legend. A spectrum analyzer can reveal non-Wi-Fi radio energy that a Wi-Fi scanner may miss. Record retransmissions, throughput, client count, and roaming events as well as signal level."],
    ["Beacons, BSSIDs, and roaming assistance", "A beacon advertises a WLAN's presence and parameters. Hiding an SSID is not a meaningful replacement for encryption and authentication. Several AP radios may advertise the same SSID or extended service set name (ESSID), but each basic service set has its own BSSID, commonly based on that radio's MAC address. A client decides when to roam; a sticky client remains on a weak AP despite a better one nearby. Supported 802.11k/v/r features can assist neighbor discovery, steering, and faster transitions. Wireless distribution systems (WDS) can link APs over wireless backhaul, but repeating consumes airtime and needs compatible, secure settings."],
    ["WPA2, WPA3, and enterprise authentication", "WPA2 with AES-CCMP or WPA3 is preferable to obsolete WEP and WPA/TKIP. WPA2-Personal uses a shared passphrase or PSK; WPA3-Personal uses SAE to improve resistance to offline password guessing. Enterprise WLANs commonly use 802.1X and EAP with a RADIUS authentication server; EAP-TLS uses certificates when the certificate lifecycle is managed correctly. A guest captive portal can present terms or a web sign-in but does not itself encrypt an open Wi-Fi link. BYOD endpoints may vary in patch and security posture, so restrict access according to device and user needs."],
    ["Wireless attacks and management frames", "A rogue AP is unauthorized equipment; an evil twin copies a trusted SSID to lure clients. Deauthentication or disassociation spoofing can disrupt clients where management frames are not protected. Protected Management Frames (PMF), required in WPA3 operation, reduce this attack surface. A matching SSID is not proof of AP identity; check BSSID, certificate validation for enterprise access, radio monitoring, and switch-port records before containment."],
    ["Coverage, channel reuse, and capacity", "Weak RSSI confined to one area suggests a coverage or AP-health problem. Strong RSSI with slow service when many users connect suggests airtime contention or AP overcapacity. In North America, channels 1, 6, and 11 are common nonoverlapping 20 MHz choices in 2.4 GHz; dense deployments may benefit from narrower channels and careful reuse rather than maximum width or power on every AP. DFS rules may affect some 5 GHz channels. Adjust AP placement, antennas, channel plan, and power from survey data, then retest throughput, security, and roaming under realistic client load."]
  ],
  13: [
    ["Choose a WAN access method", "A leased line provides dedicated connectivity with a defined service level; DSL, cable, and fiber broadband differ in available speed, upload symmetry, reach, and local availability. Fiber to the premises reaches the customer site, while fiber to the curb ends nearer the site and uses another last segment. The customer-edge device connects organizational equipment to the provider. Compare measured throughput, latency, reliability, SLA, installation time, and cost for the actual location rather than choosing solely by advertised peak speed."],
    ["Remote users and site connections", "A client VPN protects an individual user's connection into an organization; a site-to-site VPN joins networks. IPsec can protect data center traffic over the public internet, with IKE negotiating security associations. Define authentication, MFA, allowed subnets, DNS behavior, and split-tunnel policy according to the use case. If a tunnel is up but applications fail, inspect routes, overlapping address ranges, firewall rules, and the actual service ports."],
    ["Host access and management paths", "SSH on TCP 22 provides encrypted command-line access; Telnet on TCP 23 sends data without encryption and should not be used on untrusted networks. RDP commonly uses TCP 3389 for a graphical Windows session. In-band management shares the production network, whereas out-of-band management uses a separate console or management path when the data plane fails. A jump box is a controlled intermediary for reaching isolated devices. If an appliance does not boot normally, a console connection can expose startup messages even when SSH is unavailable."],
    ["APIs and secure automation", "A network API lets software read state or submit configuration requests, often over HTTPS. Use scoped credentials, TLS, authorization, and logging; do not store production secrets in scripts. A successful API response should be checked against the intended device state. APIs are useful for repeated tasks, but an automated change still needs review, a test plan, and a rollback path. A jump box or management network can limit where automation is allowed to run."]
  ],
  14: [
    ["Data center traffic and storage", "North-south traffic enters or leaves a data center; east-west traffic moves between workloads. A spine-and-leaf fabric gives predictable paths between leaf switches and can reduce the number of network hops between servers. A storage area network (SAN) exposes block storage over a dedicated or carefully managed network; Fibre Channel is one transport used in SANs. East-west visibility matters because two compromised VMs may communicate without crossing the perimeter firewall."],
    ["Cloud service and deployment models", "Public cloud uses provider-operated shared infrastructure; private cloud dedicates the environment to one organization; hybrid links environments. IaaS supplies compute, storage, and network resources while the customer manages more of the operating system and applications. PaaS also manages the application platform; SaaS delivers a finished application. Greater customer control usually means greater management responsibility, though actual cost depends on architecture and contract. A service-level agreement (SLA) defines promised availability and remedies; read its exact scope."],
    ["Virtual networks and exposure", "A VPC or similar cloud network contains subnets and route tables. An internet gateway can connect a routed public subnet to the internet, while private subnets may use a NAT gateway for outbound access without directly accepting inbound connections. Security groups commonly apply to instances or interfaces, while subnet security lists apply at a broader boundary. Check both network policy and workload firewall rules. VMs run guest operating systems; containers share the host kernel, so isolation and patch responsibilities differ."],
    ["Scalability, microservices, and CDN", "Scalability means a system can handle growth; elasticity means resources expand and contract with demand. Microservices divide an application into smaller services that can scale and fail independently, but they also create more east-west traffic and operational dependencies. A CDN stores copies of content near users to reduce distance and origin load. Plan capacity, observability, and recovery for each component rather than assuming that putting a workload in the cloud guarantees availability."],
    ["Infrastructure as Code and overlays", "Infrastructure as Code stores a declarative or scripted infrastructure definition in source control so deployments can be reviewed, repeated, and compared. An ad hoc script may automate one step without defining the entire desired environment. SDN separates control decisions from forwarding. VXLAN builds a logical overlay across an IP underlay. SD-WAN selects paths according to policy and measured WAN conditions; Zero Trust evaluates access by identity and context; SASE combines connectivity and cloud-delivered security services."],
    ["IaaS migration and microservices", "In the supplied assessment's migration comparison, IaaS is the option with the most initial customer provisioning because the organization must configure virtual machines, storage, networking, and operating systems; PaaS and SaaS shift more of that setup to the provider. This is a responsibility comparison, not a universal price rule: actual up-front costs depend on contract and design. Microservices split a monolithic application into smaller independently deployable services, which can improve scaling and compartmentalization, though they create more dependencies and do not automatically make debugging easier."]
  ]
};
